<h1>Ohio's electric bill rose 9.4% — and about $16 a month of it has a name</h1>
<p class="stand">Ohio's residential rate reached 17.79 cents a kilowatt-hour in August 2026. About $16 a month of an AEP Ohio bill is PJM capacity charge, and roughly 63% of that is attributable to data center load. The state approved a 25-megawatt tariff in July. It applies to new load, so it did not touch this year's bill.</p>
<p class="byline"><span>By <a href="../authors/krishna-basnet.html">Krishna Basnet</a></span><span>·</span><span>Manassas, Virginia</span><span>·</span><time datetime="2026-11-20">20 November 2026</time></p>

<div class="box"><dl>
<div><dt>Rate, Aug 2026</dt><dd>17.79¢/kWh</dd></div>
<div><dt>Change, year on year</dt><dd>+9.4%</dd></div>
<div><dt>National rank</dt><dd>29th cheapest of 51</dd></div>
<div><dt>Capacity pass-through</dt><dd>~$16/month</dd></div>
<div><dt>Large-load tariff</dt><dd>25 MW, approved July 2026</dd></div>
<div><dt>Attributable to data centers</dt><dd>~63% of the pass-through</dd></div>
</dl></div>

<p><strong>Ohio's increase is capacity, not generation.</strong> PJM Interconnection's capacity auction cleared roughly ten times higher between mid-2024 and mid-2025, and the pass-through landed on AEP Ohio bills at about $16 a month. PJM's independent market monitor named data centers the primary cause; consumer advocates attributed 63% of the increase specifically to data center load.</p>

<p>Ohio is the clearest case in the country of a state that both caused the problem and moved first on the fix. AEP Ohio's large-load tariff — 25 megawatts, approved at the Public Utilities Commission of Ohio in July 2026 — was the earliest of its kind. It is also why Ohio's own rise is still 9.4%: a tariff set in July cannot reprice an auction already cleared.</p>

<h2>Why Ohio's tariff didn't help this year</h2>

<p>Three reasons, and they apply to every state that has done this.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Timing.</strong> The 2025/26 and 2026/27 auctions cleared before the tariff existed. The charge is already embedded in supply rates through mid-2029.</li>
<li><strong>New load only.</strong> A tariff binds projects that interconnect after it takes effect. The campuses already drawing from the AEP zone are not repriced.</li>
<li><strong>Design.</strong> A 25 MW floor catches hyperscalers and mid-size colocation. It does not catch the 12-megawatt facility two towns over.</li>
</ul>

<p>The honest summary, which the industry does not like: a tariff is a brake, not a reverse gear. Six states with published figures averaged +7.0% over the year. Five PJM states without one averaged +7.6%. The gap is six-tenths of a point and inside the noise.</p>

<h2>What Ohio did right</h2>

<p>Ohio's statute went further than most on one point: it writes residential and commercial protection into the text rather than leaving it to the commission's discretion. That is the difference between an instrument with teeth and a press release. <a href="state-data-center-tariff-tracker.html">The tracker</a> carries the citation.</p>

<p>Virginia, by contrast, went further on security: $1.5 million a megawatt in collateral, a 14-year term, 85% of the transmission demand covered. <a href="virginia-gs5-tariff-data-centers.html">Full terms here</a>.</p>

<h2>What to do if your bill jumped</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Get the line items.</strong> AEP Ohio must itemise on request. Check whether the rise sits in the capacity line, the riders, or the fixed charge — they have different remedies.</li>
<li><strong>Compare to last August.</strong> Month-to-month is noise. Year-on-year is the number the table uses.</li>
<li><strong>Check the PUCO docket.</strong> Ohio has an open large-load docket. Public comment is free.</li>
<li><strong>Check the fixed charge.</strong> A rate held flat while the customer charge rises is a rate increase in disguise.</li>
</ul>

<section class="faq">
<h2>Questions</h2>
<details><summary>Why did my Ohio electric bill go up?</summary><p class="ans">About $16 a month of an AEP Ohio bill is PJM capacity charge, and roughly 63% of that is attributable to data center load. The rest is fuel and the general capacity clearing price.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does Ohio's 25 MW tariff help my bill?</summary><p class="ans">Not this year. It was approved in July 2026 and applies to new load, so it slows future rises rather than reversing an auction already cleared. Ohio still rose 9.4%.</p></details>
<details><summary>How much did Ohio electricity rise?</summary><p class="ans">9.4% in the year to August 2026, from 16.26 to 17.79 cents a kilowatt-hour — the fourth-largest mainland rise after Maryland, Massachusetts and Maine.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is Ohio electricity expensive?</summary><p class="ans">About average. 17.79 cents against a national average of 18.16, ranking 29th cheapest of 51 jurisdictions. The problem is the rate of change, not the level.</p></details>
</section>

